     "derivation": "Kent p.202 has only the derivative: 'māniya- sb., probably \"personal property\" in the houses; nt. adj. as collective sb., from māna-, GAv. demāna-, Av. nmāna- \"abode, house\" (§126), extension of root seen in Skt. dáma- \"house\", Gk. dómos, Lt. domus', attested once, DB 1.65 (māniyam-cā, among the things Gaumāta had taken from the people and Darius restored). So (a) OP *māna- 'house' is a reconstruction of Kent's, not an attested Old Persian lexeme with its own entry; (b) the attested word māniya- means 'household property, chattels', not 'domestic, house servant' - Kent explicitly notes the divergent interpretations (Tolman Lex. 116, Bartholomae AiW 1168 etc.). The derivation from the root is certain, the forms and glosses as Nourai prints them are only approximately Kent's; hence 'plausible'. Mo'in, incidentally, cites the same word as 'پارسی باستان māniya- (خانه، سرای)' in the Borhān footnote to مان.",
